              "content": "Codex",
              "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "icon",
          "id": "P6qSdL",
          "x": 884,
          "y": 256,
          "name": "cursor_grab",
          "width": 16,
          "height": 16,
          "icon": "hand",
          "library": "lucide",
          "fill": "$text-hi"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "D1jHp",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 462,
          "name": "cap_b_note",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 1432,
          "content": "Four drops on every other pane: left, right, up, down — the nearest edge under the pointer, so the pane is cut into four triangles by its diagonals. The highlight is the preview: the half the dragged pane will occupy, tinted accent at 12 % with a 1 px accent inset. A drop is a move — the dragged pane leaves its split as × would, the target splits on that side, 50 / 50. No center drop and no swap; a swap is two drags. A side that would break the 240 × 160 px floor shows no preview. Over itself, the drawer, or outside the tab body: no preview, and release changes nothing.",
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 10,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "WptMX",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 520,
          "name": "cap_rule",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "THE RULE",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "u8yseR",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 542,
          "name": "notes_rule",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "THE GRIP — lucide grip-horizontal centered in the identity line's free space, on every pane of a split, chat or terminal. Faint at rest so it reads as texture; text tone on hover with the open-hand cursor; closed hand while dragging, a pill with the pane's icon and name under the pointer. Only the grip drags: the name still renames, the icons still press. Not hover-only — the line already carries resting chrome, and a handle that appears from nowhere is what confused Ghostty's users.\n\nFOUR DROPS, EACH A MOVE — left, right, up, down by the nearest edge of the pane under the pointer; the preview is the half the dragged pane will occupy. The dragged pane leaves its split (its sibling takes the space, as × leaves it) and the target is split on that side with the dragged pane as the new sibling at 50 / 50; the rest of the tree keeps its sizes. No center drop, no swap — a swap is two drags. Leaves move with their ids and the pane element is keyed by id, so no terminal remounts. After the drop the dragged pane keeps focus, the tree persists, and the sidebar row's title recomposes.\n\nUNCHANGED — gutter resize, ⌘[ / ⌘], the split icon and shortcuts, ×, ⋯, sessions, drawers, the mission workspace, the sidebar's own drag-reorder. NOT NOW — drag out to a new tab and drag a sidebar chat in (the next spec, same handle); fork-to-split-pane; keyboard reorder; undo (drag it back).",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 12,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "Y0wR8j",
          "x": 756,
          "y": 520,
          "name": "cap_code",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "WHAT CHANGES IN CODE",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "w0gD76",
          "x": 756,
          "y": 542,
          "name": "notes_code",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "MODEL — PaneLayout::move_to(pane, target, side) removes the leaf through the × collapse path and splits the target on that side with the moved leaf as b (or a for left / top); ids survive. drop_side(pane_bounds, pointer) → Side (nearest edge by the diagonals), drop_preview(pane_bounds, side) → Bounds, and the floor check as pure functions; tests on a three-pane tree for every side, ids and sizes preserved, the floor.\n\nDRAG — a PaneDrag { tab_id, pane_id } value on the grip's on_drag, the pill as the drag view (the SplitResizeDrag pattern in panes.rs); on_drag_move on each pane computes the side from the pointer and stores it on the root, the preview draws from that state; on_drop on the pane applies it, plus a fallback on_drop on the tab body for the pixels a pane's drop hitbox does not cover (the GPUI gotcha the sidebar already handles). grip-horizontal.svg joins assets.rs; CursorStyle::OpenHand on hover, ClosedHand while dragging.\n\nDOCS — the 64 smoke test's chrome section; docs/arch where it describes pane order.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 12,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "frame",
      "id": "TbPja",
      "x": 8520,
      "y": 1693,
      "name": "Spec — Terminal tab split (574) · v1",
      "width": 1500,
      "height": 830,
      "fill": "$bg",
      "layout": "none",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "OCVKk",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 20,
          "name": "spec_title",
          "fill": "$text-hi",
          "content": "Spec — Terminal tab split (574) · v1",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 14,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "ItMSm",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 42,
          "name": "spec_sub",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 1452,
          "content": "Spec 570 wrote “a split holds chats only”, so a terminal tab hides the split icon and ⌘D does nothing there. That rule was about mixing, not about terminals. A terminal tab splits like a chat tab — same icon, same menu, same tree, same floor — and the new pane is a shell at once, in the cwd of the pane it came from, the way Ghostty splits. #574.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "Zf5BT",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 82,
          "name": "cap_a",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "A · TODAY — A TERMINAL TAB HIDES THE SPLIT ICON, AND ⌘D DOES NOTHING",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
