      "x": 13380,
      "y": 1693,
      "name": "Spec — Session status (347) · v1 · single-pane tab",
      "clip": true,
      "width": 1500,
      "height": 790,
      "fill": "$bg",
      "layout": "none",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "mxLSf",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 20,
          "name": "spec_title",
          "fill": "$text-hi",
          "content": "Spec — Hook-based session status (347) · v1 · single-pane tab",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 14,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "k0GxB",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 42,
          "name": "spec_sub",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 1452,
          "content": "Most tabs hold one pane, and a one-pane tab has no pane header at all — pane identity appears only from two panes up, so the pane's title and its status dot do not exist. Today that chat shows no status anywhere on the chat surface; the sidebar's working spinner is the only signal, and it cannot say Needs you. Status therefore belongs in the tab bar whenever the tab has one pane: same glyph, same label, same tooltip, placed after the truncating title and before the kebab so the controls never move. Splitting hands status down to the pane headers and the tab bar drops it — every pane is on screen, so a rollup there would only repeat what the headers already say.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "MDmvv",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 124,
          "name": "cap",
          "fill": "$text-low",
