                  "fontWeight": "500"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "jvxeP",
              "name": "note_g",
              "fill": "$text-mid",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Stripping is for agent runtimes, where a leading braille run (U+2800–U+28FF) is an animation frame rather than a word — the same range classify_title already keys on, so status and label never disagree. A shell has no spinner convention: whatever precmd or the prompt writes is the title, and Runner shows it unchanged. Two consecutive frames that strip to the same string are one update, not two.",
              "lineHeight": 1.5,
              "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "fvCru",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 1200,
          "name": "cap_rule",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "RULES",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "opKC4",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 1222,
          "name": "notes_rule",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "A name the user typed always wins. Renaming a chat pins the label, and the child may go on writing titles we no longer show; clearing the name hands the slot back to the live title.\n\nTwo fields, two authors. sessions.title and nodes.name are the user's; sessions.live_title is the process's. Which column a string sits in is what says who wrote it, so no flag records provenance and no rename dialog ever prefills with a machine's words.\n\nThe live title fills the slot nobody claimed — no user name, so the child's words stand; no title yet, or a title reset, so the runtime's own name stands.\n\nA grouped tab is named by nodes.name while it stays grouped, and by its first pane in layout order when it has no name. Moving focus inside a tab never relabels it: a tab you cannot find in the rail is worse than a tab named after only half of itself.\n\nA terminal tab — every pane a shell, which tab_is_terminal already decides — takes the shell's own title verbatim, the way every other terminal emulator does. If that turns out to be noise, #575's cwd is the better label, not a filter.\n\nMission surfaces keep the handle. @builder stays @builder for a whole run and the live title reaches the tooltip only: identity is the thing you address, so it may not move under you.\n\nAn exited pane keeps its last title, dimmed. The terminal is still on screen behind the ended card, so the title it earned is still the truest label it has — and the card, not the label, is what says the process is gone.\n\nThe persisted title is the last meaningful one, not the first, and it is written for agent runtimes only. It is cleared when the child resets the title and when a session resumes into a new conversation — a row must not go on describing work that is over.\n\nA title is never status. #347 owns Working / Needs you / Ready; a child that animates its title changes the label's words and nothing about the glyph, the unread state, the inbox, or routing.\n\nTruncation belongs to the surface. The string arrives whole, each row clips it to its own width, and identical consecutive updates coalesce into a single repaint.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 10,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "iDyMl",
          "x": 756,
          "y": 1200,
          "name": "cap_code",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "IMPLEMENTATION",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "UcwDe",
          "x": 756,
          "y": 1222,
          "name": "notes_code",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "Event::Title and Event::ResetTitle already arrive on the terminal event thread (crates/runner-terminal/src/terminal.rs:414) and land in TerminalSession.title. The accessor title() exists and has no callers today — the live half of this feature is that accessor plus a change notification.\n\nPersistence is one nullable column: ALTER TABLE sessions ADD COLUMN live_title TEXT, as 0022_session_live_title.sql, registered after line 168 of db.rs. That matches 0016, 0017 and 0019, which all add nullable session fields; 0021's side table was for coupled fields with a backfill. No index, no default, no backfill — NULL means nothing was ever reported. Surface it on DirectSessionEntry beside title.\n\nThe title belongs to the session because the process does: one PTY, one title. Tabs run nothing, so nodes needs no counterpart — session_label is the single point every surface already goes through, and a grouped tab reaches it through its first leaf.\n\nRetention on exit is free: the pane renders the dead terminal at 0.45 opacity under the ended card (panes.rs:1712), so the TerminalSession and its title are still in memory. Keep the string instead of clearing it.\n\nWrite after the dedupe, never per frame, and coalesce the burst before it reaches the view the way cmux does. Push, don't poll: raise the existing terminal repaint path when the stripped title changes.\n\nResolve in one function: nodes.name for a grouped tab, then sessions.title, then the live in-memory title, then sessions.live_title, then the derived default. PaneLayout.name is already None whenever a tab is ungrouped (pane_layout.rs:400), so the grouped-only rule needs no new condition.\n\nRuntime decides the stripping: Runtime::Shell panes take the title verbatim, agent runtimes strip the leading run first. tab_is_terminal in chat.rs already answers that for a whole tab.\n\nWhen the rename editor is open, snapshot the label it opened with — an OSC update mid-edit must not overwrite what is being typed (orca's gotcha).\n\nVerify with recorded OSC 0 and OSC 2 fixtures, BEL and ST terminators, and a sequence split across two PTY reads; a relaunch that restores the last title; the clears on reset and on a new conversation; then a live Codex session on macOS and on native Windows.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 10,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "TJNSt",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 886,
          "name": "cap_h",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "H · THE PERSISTED TITLE — ONE NULLABLE COLUMN BESIDE THE NAME YOU MIGHT TYPE, SO A CHAT IS STILL CALLED SOMETHING AFTER A RELAUNCH",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
