        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "sqD4w",
          "x": 756,
          "y": 1100,
          "name": "note_h",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 714,
          "content": "Last meaningful title wins, not the first: the agent re-emits a title only once it is working again, so the most recent one is the only thing there is to show at launch. Agent runtimes only — persisting a shell's title would strand a stale topic on a bare prompt. Cleared when the child resets the title and when a session resumes into a new conversation, so the row never describes work that is over.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 10,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "hMvGu",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 1090,
          "name": "prior_art",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "PRIOR ART — all three keep the machine's title in a field of its own. Termio persists liveTitle beside givenTitle and says so twice in comments: it once held both in one field, told them apart by pattern-matching, and froze a row's label for the rest of its life (TermioStore.swift:1770). Termio also refuses a title that is empty, starts with / or ~, contains @, or merely repeats the agent name or the folder — and labels plain terminals from OSC 7's cwd rather than the title, on the grounds that a shell reports user@host noise. Orca resolves customTitle → quickCommand → meaningful live title → vault → generated → raw live title, and snapshots the title when its rename editor opens so OSC churn cannot overwrite what is being typed. cmux runs title updates through a 1/30s burst coalescer and flushes it before any snapshot is persisted.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 10,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "frame",
      "id": "Up7D0",
      "x": 19860,
      "y": 1693,
      "name": "Spec — Provider icons for chats (593) · v1",
      "clip": true,
      "width": 1500,
      "height": 1610,
      "fill": "$bg",
      "layout": "none",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "DdABo",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 20,
          "name": "spec_title",
          "fill": "$text-hi",
          "content": "Spec — Provider icons for chats (593) · v1",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 14,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "mSKlF",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 42,
          "name": "spec_sub",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 1452,
          "content": "Every agent chat is drawn with the same speech bubble today, so a sidebar of mixed work reads as one undifferentiated stack until you read the titles. This spec gives Claude Code and Codex their own marks, taken from the runtime already stored on the session. The marks are identity, not state: neither one answers to selection, focus, or status, and the row's own fill, border, and label carry those as they do now. GPUI paints an SVG as a single-colour mask, so each mark is one flat tint written at the call site — Claude keeps its brand orange, Codex takes the theme's own foreground, which is how it stays legible on a dark ground and on a light one.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "xkriA",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 100,
          "name": "cap_a",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "A · THE MARKS — ONE PATH EACH, ONE TINT EACH, DRAWN AT THE SIZES THE APP ACTUALLY USES",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
